<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What is a cataract?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"<p>A cataract is a clouding of the natural lens of the eye. The lens is about the size of an M&amp;M and sits just behind the colored iris. At birth the lens is clear; over time it clouds and vision gets blurry. A cataract can progress until there is a complete loss of vision in the eye, and neither diet nor laser treatment will make a cataract go away. Cataract surgery can restore your vision long before the loss becomes significant enough to interfere with daily activities.</p>"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"I have astigmatism. Do I have options?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"<p>Yes. About half the population is born with corneas shaped in a way that creates astigmatism, which normally means glasses or contact lenses for clear distance vision. A toric IOL can correct, or at least partially correct, naturally occurring corneal astigmatism, so you are less dependent on glasses for distance vision after cataract surgery.</p>"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is presbyopia, and what are my choices?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"<p>Presbyopia is part of normal aging and happens to everyone. It is caused by the natural lens losing flexibility and the focusing muscle weakening, and it becomes noticeable around age 45 &mdash; the point at which most people start wearing reading glasses or bifocals.</p><p>A standard monofocal IOL does not treat presbyopia, so patients who choose one still need reading glasses afterward. Presbyopia-correcting lenses, such as multifocal and trifocal IOLs, allow most patients to see far away and up close without glasses.</p>"}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Will I still need glasses after surgery?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"<p>That depends on the lens you choose. A monofocal lens gives